INT. BRIGHTWATER ANIMAL CLINIC - EXAM ROOM - DAY

CLOSE ON a stainless steel table, a tabby cat on a towel, a stethoscope sliding across its ribs. Fluorescent hum.

DR. PRIYA NAIR, 34, sharp-eyed, hair in a hasty knot, scrubs with a faded cartoon dog on the pocket, works the stethoscope while TOMAS REYES, 36, a carpenter in a flannel shirt with sawdust still in the seams, holds the cat's head with both hands as if it were a bird's egg, and the whole small white room, the poster of canine dental anatomy, the jar of tongue depressors, the window onto a parking lot where rain is just beginning, waits for her verdict.

PRIYA
Heart's fine. Lungs are clear. She's just old, Tomas.

TOMAS
She's thirteen.

PRIYA
That's old for a cat who eats my pens.

ANGLE ON Tomas. He almost laughs.

TOMAS
She ate one pen.

PRIYA
It was a Montblanc.

INSERT - THE CAT'S CHART. Name: BISCUIT. Owner: T. REYES. Last visit: eleven months ago. A sticky note in Priya's handwriting reads, "Ask him about the dinner."

BACK TO SCENE

Priya sees him see it. She flips the chart closed. This is the last time they will be alone in this room, though neither of them knows it yet.

TOMAS
You wrote that after I cancelled.

PRIYA
I write a lot of things.

TOMAS
Priya.

PRIYA
It's fine. I got over it. I have a very busy schedule of getting over things.

WE SEE her hand shaking just slightly as she caps the stethoscope, and him noticing, and neither of them saying so.

TOMAS
My mother was sick. That's why I cancelled. I should have called.

PRIYA
You did call. Three months later.

TOMAS
I know.

A beat. Biscuit purrs, loud, unashamed.

PRIYA
Is your mother okay?

TOMAS
She passed in March.

Priya's face changes. All the armor goes out of it at once.

PRIYA
Tomas. I'm so sorry.

TOMAS
I wasn't telling you for sympathy. I was telling you because you should have been told. A long time ago.

The door swings open without a knock and WENDY OKAFOR, 20s, vet tech, bleach-blond ponytail, a clipboard hugged to her chest and a golden retriever puppy on a leash tugging her sideways, pulls up short at the sight of the two of them standing a foot apart.

WENDY
Oh. Sorry. Dr. Nair, Mrs. Halloran's here with the Great Dane and she says it's an emergency but it's the same emergency as Tuesday.

PRIYA
Give me two minutes.

WENDY
She said that's what you said on Tuesday.

PRIYA
Wendy.

WENDY
Going. I'm going.

The puppy scrambles in a circle, tangling the leash around Tomas's boots. He crouches to free it, and Wendy stands there a second too long, reading the room the way a young person reads a room, with enormous obviousness.

WENDY (CONT'D)
(to the puppy)
Come on, Waffles. Let's give the grown-ups the room.

She tows the puppy out. The door clicks. CLOSE ON the doorknob, still turning, then still.

Tomas stands with a tangle of leash in his hand that no one is holding anymore.

TOMAS
Have dinner with me. Not a date. Just dinner. I'll pay and I'll answer anything you ask.

PRIYA
Anything?

TOMAS
Anything.

PRIYA
Why did you take eleven months to bring her back in?

TOMAS
Because every time I picked up the carrier I thought about walking in here and seeing you, and I didn't know what face I had.

Silence. Rain on the window now, steady. Biscuit stretches a paw toward the edge of the table and Priya catches her without looking.

PRIYA
Thursday. I get off at seven.

TOMAS
Thursday.

PRIYA
Bring the cat. She's the only one who ever told me the truth about you.

He smiles, finally, a real one, tired and relieved, and scoops Biscuit into the carrier as Priya holds the door. For a moment they both stand in the doorway, too close, the narrow frame making them one shape, and in the hallway behind them Mrs. Halloran's Great Dane begins, mournfully, to howl.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
That's my emergency.

TOMAS
Go.

She goes. He stays a moment longer in the empty exam room, looking at the wet window, at the last line of the chart, at the sticky note he has not been supposed to read, and he is smiling at nothing.
